00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.360
what the working code does that has its use uses.

95
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.360
That's very good.

96
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.640
If you want to refactor

97
00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:35.520
the code, do behavior preserving change and not break anything.

98
00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:36.880
But it's rubbish.

99
00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.880
If you want to develop a new system and verify that this new system

100
00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.960
continues to do or does the right things, because now it's proving

101
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.080
that it does the wrong thing because that's where we started from.

102
00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.640
So that's not giving us our ability and ability

103
00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.080
to define what we really wanted.

104
00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.680
AI generated tests.

105
00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.680
If the code is the only input, we can only verify that the code remains the same.

106
00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:07.800
We can't infer the goals from the solution because the solution can always

107
00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:08.680
be wrong.

108
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.120
So they're mostly a dumb idea.

109
00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.760
They have a they have a place, but mostly a dumb idea.

110
00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.200
They tend to be a cop out

111
00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.200
for people who don't can't be bothered to state their goals.

112
00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:25.680
And I think that's a problem

113
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.200
because specifying the goals is kind of what our job is.

114
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.160
It's kind of what we're here to do as software developers.

115
00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.200
My next question what's the program for?

116
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.640
To define a sequence of instructions.

117
00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:47.800
Well not really. That's not the goal.

