00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:53.040
It might be what it is, but to encode algorithms, that's not the goal.

119
00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.000
Again of of a program.

120
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.400
That's not what we do it for.

121
00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.400
That's not the value that it brings

122
00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.840
to implement our brilliant design.

123
00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:03.560
Again,

124
00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:08.440
that's some of the self-fulfillment that I as a programmer might get from it.

125
00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:11.400
But that's not the goal. That's that's not the goal.

126
00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.440
That's not the reason that people pay me to do it.

127
00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:16.800
So the

128
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.280
commercial pressure is not none of those things.

129
00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:21.800
That's not really what it's for.

130
00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:25.560
As much as we might might like to think of it in that way as programmers.

131
00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:30.200
Programing languages, I would argue, have three goals as tools.

132
00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.760
So first, they are tools

133
00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.840
that have been designed to help us to organize our thinking about a problem.

134
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.240
They allow us to explore the surface area of a problem

135
00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.840
and kind of understand it in more detail, in more depth,

136
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.960
and they're designed to work that way.

137
00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.760
Programing languages aren't difficult because we want it

138
00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.680
to be obscure in a truce.

139
00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:59.240
We wanted tools that would help us to explore the problems in these kind of way.

140
00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.520
They're designed to help us to do that.

141
00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.880
They're also a means of

142
00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.720
communicating our understanding with other programmers or the humans.

143
00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.880
So they are communication tool between us, so we can work as part of a team

144
00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.680
and understand what we are in some technical detail,

145
00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.560
exploring the depth and the breadth and the complexity of a of

146
00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.000
of the problem that we're trying to solve and the solution

147
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.680
that we've come up with encoded as a program in a programming language.

148
00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.640
And ultimately they're there to tell a computer what to do.

149
00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.000
But that's kind of the last part, because assembly language

150
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.280
instructions inform a computer what to do perfectly well.

151
00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.840
And we not many of us, spend our time writing programs

152
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.840
in assembly language.

153
00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:56.200
There are also three techniques that are embodied in programing.

154
00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:00.400
Languages that are think are important for us to think about and to remember

155
00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.720
when we're talking about programing as a discipline.

156
00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.520
The first is we have a simple, consistent grammar.

157
00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:12.560
It allows us to express our ideas

158
00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:16.320
in a concrete, specific, precise way,

159
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.080
precisely enough to be executable by a computer.

160
00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.920
They are an unambiguous expression

161
00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:27.840
of our intent in that respect and that this, this matters.

162
00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.800
And they're repeatable and deterministic in terms of execution.

163
00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.600
And this matters a lot too.

164
00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.800
If we write something in the programming language of our choice

165
00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.880
and run it twice, given the same inputs, we're going to get the same result.

166
00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:47.880
But every time that's important, it means that we can reason about it.

167
00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.080
It means that we can test it.

168
00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.040
It means that we can understand what it means,

169
00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:56.080
and we can design systems that are more or less repeatable in that sense.

170
00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.080
But the tools themselves are repeatable.

171
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.680
How does natural language measure up to that?

172
00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:08.960
Because natural language, somebody said today, I think they quoted Andrei

173
00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.360
Karpathy saying that the programing language of the future is English.

174
00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.960
I don't like that idea very much for the reasons that I'm talking about.

175
00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:21.080
It's not precise enough, it's too vague.

176
00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.120
Natural language helps us.

177
00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.120
How does that measure up?

178
00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.120
Helps us to organize our thinking about a problem?

179
00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.480
Well, it's too vague, really.

180
00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.920
Natural language is open to misinterpretation.

181
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.280
If anybody's been around.

182
00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.880
I can't really see you very well.

183
00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:42.080
But if you've got a gray beard like mine and you've been around enough,

184
00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:48.400
I bet that you've been in a situation where somebody coming down from on high

185
00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:53.040
and given a very vague instruction to a bunch of programmers and gone away

186
00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.200
and then been really disappointed in the results

187
00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.400
because they didn't tell us in enough detail, that's really common.

188
00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.880
It's really easy to misinterpret what the goal really is.

189
00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:07.720
Natural

190
00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:10.840
language is always open to interpretation.

191
00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:12.720
Time flies like an arrow.

192
00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:13.920
These are some time flies.

193
00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.920
Liking arrows.

194
00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:23.040
Vibe coding alone is simply not good enough.

195
00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:27.960
If we're just chatting with the computer to express our needs, that's not enough.

196
00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:29.200
We need tools

197
00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:33.760
that allow us to be more precise than that, to be more specific than that,

198
00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:37.000
to be better prompts of what it is that we want to achieve.

199
00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.800
That doesn't mean that we need to look at all the code,

200
00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:45.440
and are only allowed to do this in a programming language.

201
00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:50.160
I'm I'm in the category of its been a long time now since I've.

202
00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:55.920
So I've written any code by hand because my AI agent writes all the code,

203
00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:01.120
but I've been more precise than just natural language in some respects.

204
00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:05.720
In specifying what I want from it, I'm able to get what I want from it

205
00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:07.560
by using a precise,

206
00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:11.440
prescriptive version of natural language in order to be able to prompt it.

207
00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:14.280
And that's what I'm really talking about here.

208
00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:18.720
So does it communicate our understanding with other humans?

209
00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:21.320
It's a bit too

210
00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.640
ambiguous to do that very well, because as we

211
00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:28.560
as we all know, does it tell a computer what we want it to do?

212
00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:30.400
It's too imprecise for that.

213
00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.560
So it's very easy unless what we're telling it

